  "interpretation": "Pooled across all seeds: 1684/1726 recall shocks were reappointed within 30 steps (pooled rate=0.9756662804171495). Seed-clustered (mean-of-per-seed-rate, n_seeds=15): reappointment_rate point=0.9746264906939245, CI=[0.9644734074453173, 0.9835692491000226]. THIS IS NOT THE SAME QUANTITY AS FUNCTIONAL RECOVERY. Among reappointed successors with a defined recovery_gap (n_seeds=15, n_gap_undefined_among_reappointed pooled=0): mean_recovery_gap (post_shock_capacity - pre_shock_capacity) point=-0.09971139484677854, CI=[-0.1112803637985707, -0.08712121365526684]. Full recovery (gap>=0, matched or exceeded pre-shock capacity) rate: point=0.32225344886500107, CI=[0.3038088263840947, 0.3454832752062024]. Within-tolerance recovery (gap>=-0.05) rate: point=0.5357884381477847, CI=[0.5203770659244947, 0.5518425225961732]. Completion-rate dimension: mean_pre_shock_completion_rate point=0.4922105763837979, mean_post_shock_completion_rate point=0.4920959113248836. If the reappointment-rate estimate above sits substantially higher than the full/within-tolerance recovery rates, that gap IS the concrete demonstration this work package was commissioned to produce: reappointment measures whether an office got refilled, not whether the office-holder's realized administrative capacity or task-completion performance actually returned to (or exceeded) its pre-shock level. Read the actual point estimates and CIs above -- including whether either interval crosses the other's -- before asserting the gap is large, small, or statistically distinguishable; this text does not assert a specific conclusion so it stays accurate regardless of which way the numbers land. See docs/level1.1b_validation_note.md for the confirmatory read."
